1000489,UR179,KH0417,Cajamarquilla,,"""American Museum of Natural History, NY""","As on B/3453A, the knots of B/3453B, C, and D are generally no-axis knots.  However, the smallest, ""single type"" knots do have axes.  
1) As on B/3453 A, th knots of B/3453 B are generally no axis knots. However, the smallest, ""single type"" knots do have axes.
In the table, I have designated knots as one or another of three types, depending on (approx) size, as follows: 
A = small, single knot (usually with axis)
B = medium (ca. 1.0 cm. long) no axis
C = large (ca. 1.5 cm. + long) no axis
(on the recording sheets, axis direction given only for type A). In the below data tables, type A, B, or C noted in the ""notes"" column of each cord.

2) cords on this sample are paired. the pairs are attached by usual lark's heard knot. 
